---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import HeroFlow from '../components/HeroFlow.astro';
import IntroVideo from '../components/IntroVideo.astro';
import Offer from '../components/Offer.astro';
import IntentTeaser from '../components/IntentTeaser.astro';
import { APP_STORE_URL } from '../consts';
import BookDemo from '../components/BookDemo.astro';

const title = 'QSortby — Collection Sorting & Upsells for Shopify';
const description =
  'Auto-sort Shopify collections on a rule you write — then check the result: why each product ranks where it does, what the last run changed, and what it earned.';
---
<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap herogrid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Turn Every Collection Into a Sales Machine — Sort Smarter, Sell More.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Real-time sorting by trending, best-selling, or your own custom rules — fully automated, always up to date.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Install free <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a>
          <BookDemo class="btn btn-line btn-lg">Book a demo</BookDemo>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-viz"><HeroFlow /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== INTRO VIDEO =====================
       High on the page — right after the trust bar, before the first wall of
       argument — because a visitor who would rather be shown than told should
       not have to hunt for it. The hero keeps its own trust bar directly beneath
       it; the video takes the next slot.

       Renders only when the asset exists (see public/video/README.md), so the
       page is complete with or without it. -->
  <IntroVideo duration="1:37" />

  <!-- ===================== WHAT YOU GET =====================
       The three jobs the app does, each with its own short case and picture —
       merchandising, personalization, upsell. Sits right under the intro
       video: whoever just watched the whole journey lands here to see the
       three buckets it came from. See src/components/Offer.astro. -->
  <Offer />

  <!-- ===================== SHOWS ITS WORK =====================
       Sits immediately after the console because the console raises the exact
       fear this answers: "so it rearranges my storefront on its own?"

       ── This is the differentiating section on the page. ──
       It was framed as "You stay in control" — a reassurance. That framing
       undersold it. Auto-sorting, sold-out demotion and rules on sales/margin/
       stock are the whole category now; a merchant comparing apps in the App
       Store sees the same bullet list on every listing. What none of them offer
       is the ability to CHECK the result — why a product landed where it did,
       what the last run changed, whether it ran at all this morning, and what
       the revenue figure actually means. Those four are the reason to choose
       this app, so they lead here rather than sitting in three separate places.

       Every claim here is a shipped, UNGATED capability, verified in the app
       repo: the per-signal breakdown on the product page (routes/app/products/
       $id.tsx, no plan guard), the before/after preview, manual approval,
       manual membership, sort snapshot + config history, the run/pixel/sync
       state on the app home, and last-touch attribution. Nothing may be added
       here unless a merchant can verify it inside the app on the Free plan. -->
  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr shead-lg rv" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <h2>If you've said any of these, you're in the right place.</h2>
        <p class="lead-c">Nobody wakes up wanting to re-order a collection. Something looked wrong, and you went looking for a way to stop fixing it by hand.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="bento rv">
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 5v13M6 13l6 6 6-6"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Sold-out products hog the front row</h3>
          <p>Shopify's default sort leaves a sold-out product right where it was, someone has to notice and move it by hand. QSortby drops it within minutes automatically, and brings it back the moment it's restocked.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l1.8 4.9L19 9.7l-4.9 1.8L12 16.4l-1.8-4.9L5 9.7l4.9-1.8z"/></svg></span>
          <h3>New drops get buried before they have a chance</h3>
          <p>A brand-new product has no sales history yet, so a best-seller sort puts it at the bottom on day one. QSortby factors in how new it is too, so it can actually rise.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="8" r="3.2"/><path d="M5 20c0-3.9 3.1-6 7-6s7 2.1 7 6"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Every visitor sees the exact same order</h3>
          <p>No matter what a shopper is looking for, the collection looks the same for everyone. QSortby builds a feed from what this visit is actually doing, no login or purchase history needed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 17l6-6 4 4 8-8"/><path d="M15 7h6v6"/></svg></span>
          <h3>You reorder a collection and have no idea if it helped</h3>
          <p>The order changes, then you wait two weeks and guess whether sales moved because of it or for ten other reasons. QSortby runs the new order as an A/B test against the old one, so you get real numbers instead of a guess.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l9 5-9 5-9-5z"/><path d="M3 13l9 5 9-5"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Merchandising breaks down past a few hundred products</h3>
          <p>Dragging products around by hand works fine with twenty items. With a thousand, it's just not possible anymore. QSortby lets you set the rule once and apply it to every collection, new products sort themselves in without you touching a thing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="b">
          <span class="ic"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2 12s4-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-4 7-10 7-10-7-10-7z"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/></svg></span>
          <h3>Nobody can explain why a product ranks where it does</h3>
          <p>Someone asks why a product is sitting where it is, and most apps just don't tell you. QSortby shows you the rule behind every ranking in plain language, and you can change it anytime.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== INTENT AI =====================
       One band, linking to /features/intent-ai. After "in the right place"
       because that section's third card ("Every visitor sees the exact same
       order") is the problem Intent AI answers. Growth-only, and says so —
       see src/components/IntentTeaser.astro. -->
  <IntentTeaser />

  <!-- ===================== FINAL CTA ===================== -->
  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta rv">
        <h2>Let your storefront optimize itself — and <em>prove it did.</em></h2>
        <p>Install free, run it on one collection, and read the attributed revenue in your own dashboard.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta" style="justify-content:center">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Install free <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a>
          <BookDemo class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">Book a demo</BookDemo>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
